<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-photosensors">
    <title>Photosensors and the Solar Tracker</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The goal of this chapter is to build a solar tracker: a servomotor that
            turns an arm carrying two light sensors until the arm points at a light
            source, and that keeps following the source as it moves.  The sensor is a
            photocell, a resistor whose resistance falls as more light falls on it.
            The ADC (analog-to-digital converter) measures voltages and not
            resistances, so the first job is an interface: a voltage divider that
            turns the photocell's resistance into a voltage on an ADC pin.  With two
            photocells on the servo's arm, one on each side, the difference between
            their two voltages depends on which side the light is on, and a program we
            write turns that difference into a change in the servo's command pulse.
            That program is a feedback loop.  The servo has its own feedback loop
            inside it, and the tracker closes a second loop around the servo.
        </p>
        <p>
            This chapter covers one class day.  Before class, the reading introduces
            the photocell, the family of sensors that work the same way, the divider,
            the photocell's datasheet, and what a solar tracker is.  In class we'll
            predict which way the divider's voltage moves as the light changes, do the
            photocell study from Lab 8, look at the other sensors the same circuit
            reads, mount the photocells on the arm, design the tracker's feedback loop
            in the notation used in Lab 8, and start on the loop.  Lab 8 is where you
            tune and demonstrate it.  <xref ref="sec-photo-reference"/> collects the
            divider formula, the two-channel ADC read and the loop's update rule for
            looking up later.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Explain what a CdS photocell is: a resistor whose resistance falls as more light falls on it, inexpensive, nonlinear, and so variable from unit to unit that it is used to compare light levels rather than to measure them.</li>
                <li>Put a resistive sensor into a voltage divider, state the divider's formula, and predict which way its output moves as the sensor's resistance changes.</li>
                <li>Read the photocell's datasheet for what the tracker needs: the sensitivity as the slope of a log-log plot, the rise and fall times as a bound on how fast to sample, and the test conditions behind every number.</li>
                <li>Recognize the same divider interface across a family of resistance-based sensors, and say which sensors need more than a divider.</li>
                <li>Mount two photocells on the servo's arm, wire both dividers to two ADC channels, and print both readings.</li>
                <li>Design the tracker's control loop from Lab 8's description: the error, the update rule, its sign, its bounds, the sampling interval, and what a gain of 0.01 means in integer arithmetic.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-photo-before-class">
        <title>Before Class: The Photocell and the Voltage Divider</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In the ADC chapter we measured a voltage from a potentiometer, and in the
                servo chapter we turned that voltage into a position.  The solar tracker
                replaces the potentiometer with a sensor that responds to light.  This
                reading covers what that sensor is, the family of sensors that work the
                same way, how a resistance becomes a voltage the ADC can measure, what the
                sensor's datasheet says, and what a solar tracker is.  In class we'll
                build the circuit and design the loop.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-cell">
            <title>What a Photocell Is</title>
            <p>
                A <term>photocell</term> is a resistor whose resistance depends on how
                much light falls on its face (<xref ref="fig-photocell-photo"/>).  It is
                also called a CdS cell, after the cadmium sulfide it is made of, a
                <term>light-dependent resistor</term> (LDR), or a photoresistor.
                Cadmium sulfide's resistivity decreases with increasing light exposure.
                A photocell is made of semiconductor material with a high resistance,
                because very few of its electrons are free to move: the vast majority are
                locked into the crystal lattice and cannot move.  As light falls on the
                semiconductor, photons are absorbed by the lattice and some of their
                energy is transferred to the electrons.  The amount of energy transferred
                gives some of the electrons sufficient energy to break free from the
                crystal lattice, so that they can then conduct electricity, and the
                resistance of the photocell falls.  The process is progressive: as more
                light shines on the photocell, more electrons are released, and the
                resistance falls further.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-photocell-photo">
                <caption>A CdS photocell.  The face is the cadmium sulfide: the dark
                meander is the sensing material, and the two posts are where the leads
                attach.  The two leads are the resistor's two terminals, and there is
                no polarity.  Image from
                <url href="https://www.adafruit.com/product/161">Adafruit</url>.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/slide03_img1.png" width="55%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Photocells are small, inexpensive, low-power, easy to use, and they do
                not wear out.  For that reason they often appear in toys, gadgets and
                appliances.  They are also very inaccurate.  Each photocell will act a
                little differently from the next, even if the two are from the same
                batch, and the variation can be really large, 50% or higher, so a
                photocell should not be used to determine a precise light level in
                <term>lux</term> (lumens per square meter, the unit of illuminance).
                Instead, you can expect to be able to determine basic light changes, and
                which of two photocells is receiving more light.  For the tracker that is
                exactly what we need, and we will not use a photocell to measure how
                bright the light is.  The
                <url href="https://learn.adafruit.com/photocells">Adafruit guide to
                photocells</url> covers the same ground and is worth reading.  Its
                wiring examples use a different board; our circuit is in
                <xref ref="subsec-photo-divider"/>.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-family">
            <title>Sensors Based on Resistance</title>
            <p>
                Many sensors are based on a variable resistance.  A potentiometer, linear
                or rotary, is one: its resistance from the wiper to either end depends on
                position or angle, which is how it gave us a voltage in the ADC chapter.
                A force-sensing resistor changes its resistance with the force pressing
                on it.  A photoresistor changes its resistance with light.  A thermistor
                changes its resistance with temperature.  The photoresistor, the
                thermistor and the force-sensing resistor are read with the same circuit,
                the voltage divider of the next subsection, and one ADC channel each; the
                potentiometer needs no fixed resistor, because its own track is both
                halves of the divider.  A strain gage senses a tiny stretch or
                compression as a very small change in resistance, and a load cell, which
                measures weight, is built from strain gages; those two require more
                complex electronics than a simple voltage divider, because the change to
                be measured is so small.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-divider">
            <title>The Voltage Divider as the Interface</title>
            <p>
                The ADC measures a voltage, and a photocell is a resistance.  The circuit
                that turns one into the other is a <term>voltage divider</term>: the
                sensor in series with a fixed resistor between the supply and ground,
                with the node between them going to an ADC pin
                (<xref ref="fig-photocell-divider"/>).  Call the sensor's resistance
                <m>R_\text{sens}</m> and the fixed resistor <m>R_M</m>.  The voltage at
                the node is
                <md number="no">
                    <mrow>V_M = V_{cc} \cdot \frac{R_M}{R_\text{sens} + R_M}</mrow>
                </md>
                which is the potentiometer's formula from the ADC chapter
                (<xref ref="fig-adc-divider"/>) with the bottom resistor in the
                numerator: there the two resistances were the two parts of one track,
                and here they are two separate parts.  <m>V_{cc}</m> is the Nucleo's
                3.3 V, because the ADC's reference is 3.3 V
                (<xref ref="subsec-adc-quantization"/>): a node fed from 3.3 V can use
                the ADC's whole range and never exceeds it, and a voltage above the
                ADC's reference is outside the pin's operating conditions.  As the light
                changes, <m>R_\text{sens}</m> changes and <m>V_M</m> changes with it;
                which way <m>V_M</m> moves follows from the formula, and in class we'll
                work that out before we build the circuit.  Lab 8 draws the same circuit
                in its Figure 1 with the photocell named <m>R_1</m>, the fixed resistor
                <m>R_2</m>, and the node, labeled <m>V_{AVR\_PCx}</m> in the drawing
                and <m>V_1</m> in the deliverables.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-photocell-divider">
                <caption>The voltage divider as the interface between a resistive
                sensor and the ADC.  The sensor, <m>R_\text{sens}</m>, drawn as a
                variable resistor, is connected from <m>V_{cc}</m> to the node, and
                the fixed resistor <m>R_M</m> from the node to ground.  The node's
                voltage <m>V_M</m> goes to an ADC pin, A0 (PA0) or A1 (PA1) on the
                Nucleo, and the formula gives it in terms of the two
                resistances.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/fig-photocell-divider.svg" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-datasheet">
            <title>The Photocell's Datasheet</title>
            <p>
                Our photocell is the PDV-P8001, and its
                <url href="external/datasheets/CdS-photocell-PDV-P8001.pdf">datasheet</url>
                is one page, so look through the whole of it.  The description says that
                these light dependent resistors are available in a wide range of
                resistance values and are packaged in a two-leaded plastic-coated
                ceramic header; the package drawing gives the dimensions, and there is no
                polarity to get wrong.  The <em>Absolute maximum rating</em> table gives
                the applied voltage (150 V), the power dissipation and the temperature
                range, none of which our 3.3 V circuit comes near.  The
                <em>Electro-optical characteristics</em> table is the one the tracker
                depends on, and its rows carry their own test conditions: the two
                resistances and the two times are measured at 10 lux from a lamp at
                2856 K (a tungsten lamp), the sensitivity is defined between 100 lux and
                10 lux at that same color temperature, and the whole table is at 23 °C.
                A number from a datasheet means what it says only under its conditions.
                The rows are the dark resistance and the illuminated resistance, which
                in Lab 8 you are asked to look up; the sensitivity, which we'll read in
                class against a plot; the spectral range, 400 to 700 nm, with the peak
                response at 520 nm, in the green, near the middle of the range the eye
                sees; and the rise time and fall time, 55 ms and 20 ms, which say how
                fast the resistance responds when the light changes.
            </p>
            <p>
                The light levels in the table are in lux, which is not a unit most of
                us have a feel for.  The Adafruit guide has a table of examples, and a
                few of its rows give the scale:
            </p>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Illuminance</cell>
                    <cell>Example</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>0.27 to 1 lux</cell><cell>full moon on a clear night</cell></row>
                <row><cell>50 lux</cell><cell>a family living room</cell></row>
                <row><cell>300 to 500 lux</cell><cell>a well-lit office</cell></row>
                <row><cell>1,000 lux</cell><cell>an overcast day</cell></row>
                <row><cell>10,000 to 25,000 lux</cell><cell>full daylight, not direct sun</cell></row>
            </tabular>
            <p>
                The datasheet's 10 lux is a dark room, and a phone's flashlight held
                close to the cell is far brighter than that.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-tracker">
            <title>What a Solar Tracker Is</title>
            <p>
                A solar tracker uses servomotors to keep a solar panel pointed at the sun
                throughout the day.  Some trackers work by attaching photosensors at the
                edges of the solar panel, and using the imbalance between the sensors
                when the light arrives from one side to control servos in elevation
                (tilting up and down) as well as in azimuth (turning side to side).  In
                Lab 8 we build a model of an azimuth-only system, one that only turns
                side to side: the servo from the servo chapter, with the arm bolted to
                its horn (<xref ref="fig-servo-leads"/>), carries a photocell at each
                end, and each photocell is set into a shielding cup that shades it from
                light arriving from the other side.  When the arm points at the light,
                the two photocells receive the same illumination; when it does not, the
                photocell on the side nearer the light receives more.  A program reads
                both photocells through the ADC and moves the servo, and in class we'll
                work out from Lab 8's description of it how that program is designed.
                Writing it is Lab 8's design challenge.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-photo">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-photo-resistance">
                <statement>
                    <p>A photocell is in a lit room, and a hand is placed over it.  What
                    happens to its resistance?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>It rises, and it stays high for as long as the cell is covered.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  With less light, fewer electrons are freed
                        from the lattice, so fewer are available to conduct and the
                        resistance rises.  It stays high until light returns.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It falls, because the cell is no longer being driven by light.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Light is what frees the electrons that conduct.  Less
                        light means fewer free electrons, which is a higher resistance,
                        not a lower one.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It rises briefly and then returns to its lit value.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The resistance depends on the light falling on the
                        cell at the time.  It does not return to a previous value while
                        the cell stays covered.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It does not change; resistance is a fixed property of the part.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>For a plain resistor that is true.  A photocell is a
                        resistor whose resistance depends on the light on its face, which
                        is what makes it a sensor.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-photo-variation">
                <statement>
                    <p>Two photocells of the same part number, from the same bag, are
                    placed side by side under the same lamp, and their resistances differ
                    by a third.  What is the most likely explanation?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Nothing is wrong: photocells vary from one unit to the next by that much, and the datasheet gives ranges rather than one value.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The Adafruit guide puts the variation at
                        50% or higher, even within a batch, and the datasheet says the
                        part is available in a wide range of resistance values.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>One of the two cells is defective.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A difference of a third is within the normal variation
                        for these parts.  A defective cell would read open, or would not
                        change with light at all.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The cells need a few minutes under the lamp before they agree.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The rise and fall times on the datasheet are tens of
                        milliseconds, so both cells respond in tens of milliseconds, not
                        minutes.  The difference is between the cells themselves.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The multimeter is inaccurate at these resistances.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A multimeter reads resistances in the kilohm range to
                        within a percent or so.  The difference is in the parts.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-photo-swapped">
                <statement>
                    <p>Suppose the divider is wired the other way around from
                    <xref ref="fig-photocell-divider"/>: the fixed resistor from
                    <m>V_{cc}</m> to the node, and the photocell from the node to ground.
                    When the light on the photocell gets brighter, what happens to
                    <m>V_M</m>?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>It falls.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  With the photocell at the bottom, the
                        formula becomes <m>V_M = V_{cc} \cdot R_\text{sens}/(R_M +
                        R_\text{sens})</m>: the photocell's resistance is now in the
                        numerator, and as it falls the fraction falls.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It rises.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Follow the formula with the two resistors exchanged,
                        with round numbers: with <m>R_M</m> at 10 kΩ, a cell at 20 kΩ
                        gives <m>3.3 \times 20/30 = 2.2</m> V, and when the light
                        brightens it to 10 kΩ, <m>3.3 \times 10/20 = 1.65</m> V.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It does not change, because the same two resistors are in series either way.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The current through the two is the same either way,
                        but <m>V_M</m> is the voltage across whichever resistor is at the
                        bottom, and that has changed.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-photo-peak">
                <statement>
                    <p>From the PDV-P8001 datasheet, at what wavelength is the photocell's
                    response strongest?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>520 nm, which is green light.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The spectral application range is 400 to
                        700 nm and the peak is at 520 nm, so the cell responds to the
                        colors the eye sees best.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>400 nm, at the violet end of its range.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>400 nm is the lower end of the spectral range.  The peak
                        is a separate row.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>700 nm, at the red end of its range.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>700 nm is the upper end of the spectral range.  The peak
                        is a separate row.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>2856 nm, in the infrared.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>2856 K is a temperature, not a wavelength: it is the
                        color temperature of the lamp used for the table's test
                        conditions.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-photo-family">
                <statement>
                    <p>Which of these sensors needs more than a voltage divider and an ADC
                    channel to be read?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>A strain gage.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  A strain gage's resistance changes by a very
                        small fraction of its value, too small a change for a divider and
                        our ADC to resolve, so strain gages and the load cells built from
                        them require more complex electronics.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>A thermistor.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A thermistor's resistance changes a great deal with
                        temperature, and a divider reads it directly.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>A force-sensing resistor.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A force-sensing resistor changes its resistance by a
                        large factor with force, and a divider reads it directly.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>A potentiometer.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A potentiometer is its own divider, which is how it gave
                        the ADC a voltage in the ADC chapter.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-photo-day16">
        <title>Day 16 In-Class: Photosensors and the Solar Tracker</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we'll put the photocell from the reading into a voltage divider and
                predict which way its voltage moves as the light changes.  Then we'll do
                the photocell study from Lab 8: the datasheet, the multimeter, the two
                dividers on the breadboard, and a program that prints both channels.
                The photocells then go into the cups on the servo's arm, and we'll design
                the feedback loop that turns two readings into a servo command.  The loop
                itself is your lab work, and we'll start on it together at the end of
                class.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1: 10 min = 1 (recall) + 5 (commit and reveal) + 2 (log-log, the sensitivity as its slope) + 2 (compare two) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day16-divider">
            <title>Part 1: The Photocell in the Divider</title>
            <p>
                In the reading we saw that a photocell's resistance falls as more light
                falls on it.  A photocell is a resistance, and the ADC measures voltages
                rather than resistances.  The circuit that turns a resistance into a
                voltage is the divider of <xref ref="fig-photocell-divider"/>, and you
                have built one before.  The potentiometer in the ADC chapter
                (<xref ref="fig-adc-divider"/>) was a divider,
                <m>V_\text{wiper} = V_\text{supply} \cdot R_2/(R_1 + R_2)</m>, the same
                formula with the bottom resistor in the numerator.  Today the top
                resistor is the photocell and the bottom one is fixed.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-direction">
                <title>As the light gets brighter</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Discuss at your table: as the light gets brighter, does
                    <m>V_M</m> increase or decrease?  Explain your answer from the
                    formula in <xref ref="fig-photocell-divider"/>.</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <p>
                <m>R_\text{sens}</m> is in the denominator.  As the light gets brighter
                <m>R_\text{sens}</m> falls, the denominator <m>R_\text{sens} + R_M</m>
                falls, and <m>V_M</m> rises.  Here is an example with round numbers
                rather than our photocell's own.  With <m>R_\text{sens}</m> at 20 kΩ,
                <m>R_M</m> at 10 kΩ and <m>V_{cc}</m> at 3.3 V,
                <m>V_M = 3.3 \times 10/30 = 1.1</m> V, and if the light brightens until
                <m>R_\text{sens}</m> is 10 kΩ, <m>V_M = 3.3 \times 10/20 = 1.65</m> V.
                More light gives more voltage.  With the two resistors exchanged, so
                that the photocell is at the bottom, it goes the other way, by the same
                formula with <m>R_\text{sens}</m> in the numerator, which was the
                reading's question.  (<m>V_{cc}</m> is 3.3 V, the ADC's reference.)
            </p>
            <p>
                The photocell's resistance is not a linear function of the illumination
                (<xref ref="fig-photocell-loglog"/>).  The figure is a log-log plot: both
                axes are logarithmic, so each labeled step along either axis, 1k to 10k
                or 10 lux to 100 lux, is a factor of ten, and a relationship of the form
                <m>R \propto E^{-S}</m>, with <m>E</m> the illumination, is a straight
                line on these axes and only on these.  The curves are different cell
                types; the guide does not say which is which.  The datasheet's
                <em>Sensitivity</em> row is the slope of that line: its typical value of
                0.6 means that ten times the light divides the resistance by
                <m>10^{0.6}</m>, about 4, and <xref ref="subsec-photo-ref-divider"/>
                has the row's definition and the sign of the slope.  If we wanted a
                quantitative result in lux we would have to implement that conversion;
                for a light-or-dark sensor, or for our tracker, we do not.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-photocell-loglog">
                <caption>Resistance against illumination for a family of CdS photocells,
                from the Adafruit guide, with one curve highlighted in red.  Both axes are
                logarithmic, and each curve is close to a straight line on these axes:
                resistance falls by roughly a constant factor for every factor of ten in
                illumination.  This is the guide's generic family, not the PDV-P8001's
                own curve.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/slide04_0c98f286.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Each photocell is a bit different, even if two are <q>the same</q> part,
                which is why the datasheet gives ranges rather than one number and says
                the part is available in a wide range of resistance values.  The tracker
                relies on the comparison of the two cells rather than on the resistance
                of either one.  When both cells receive the same illumination, two cells
                that share the same curve give the same voltage, whatever the shape of
                that curve, so the nonlinearity does not matter for finding the balance
                point.  What the comparison does not cancel is the difference between the
                two cells themselves: two cells that differ by tens of percent balance at
                a point where the light is not quite centered on the arm.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2: 38 min = 3 (servo check, pot out) + 6 (D1-D3) + 5 (D4-D6) + 10 (the two
             dividers, AD2 voltmeter) + 1 (checkpoint at minute 39) + 11 (the two-channel
             program, D7-D8) + 2 (checkpoint at minute 53, a hard stop) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day16-lab">
            <title>Part 2: The Photocell Study from Lab 8</title>
            <p>
                We'll now work through section 2 of Lab 8, <em>Technical Study of
                Photocells</em>, with the handout in front of you; the handout has a
                space for each answer.  We'll go in the handout's order, with one change:
                we read the nodes on the AD2 before the program rather than after.
                Before the photocells go on the breadboard we'll take the potentiometer
                off, because its channel, A0 (PA0), is where the first photocell's
                divider goes.  The servo stays wired as it is.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-servo-check">
                <title>Check the servo, then remove the potentiometer</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-servo-follow">
                    <statement><p>Plug in the USB cable first, then the power adapter into
                    the regulator board, and turn the knob.  Your servo program from Day
                    15 is still on the Nucleo, and the servo rotates as you adjust the
                    potentiometer, with everything wired as in
                    <xref ref="fig-servo-powering"/>.  Note which way the arm turns as
                    the knob raises the reading; the loop needs that fact later today.
                    If the servo does not follow the knob, the symptom list in
                    <xref ref="subsec-day15-power"/> says where to look, and the study
                    and the arm wiring that follow do not depend on the servo, so go on
                    either way.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-pot-out">
                    <statement><p>Unplug the adapter from the regulator board, then the
                    USB cable from the Nucleo.  Take the potentiometer off the breadboard
                    with its three wires.  Nothing else changes: the servo's three leads
                    and the regulator board stay where they are.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                We'll start with the datasheet and the multimeter.  In Lab 8,
                Deliverable 4 asks for a fixed resistor at the <term>geometric
                mean</term> of the photocell's dark and illuminated resistances,
                <m>R_2 = \sqrt{R_\text{dark} \times R_\text{illuminated}}</m>, computed
                from the values you measure.  The mid-value is what puts the dark reading
                and the lit reading farthest apart on the ADC's scale
                (<xref ref="subsec-photo-ref-divider"/> has the reason).  The kit has
                10 kΩ resistors, so Deliverable 6 is whether the kit's 10 kΩ, rather
                than the computed value, is a problem.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-study">
                <title>Lab 8 section 2, Deliverables 1 to 6</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>The first photocell of the two in your kit is <m>R_1</m> in Lab 8's
                    Figure 1.  If your arm already carries its photocells in their cups,
                    use the alligator clips to bring the leads of one of them to the
                    multimeter and, below, to the breadboard.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d1">
                    <statement><p>Deliverable 1: look up the minimum dark resistance and the
                    minimum illuminated resistance in the PDV-P8001
                    <url href="external/datasheets/CdS-photocell-PDV-P8001.pdf">datasheet</url>.
                    Both are rows of the <em>Electro-optical characteristics</em> table;
                    note the test conditions beside each.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d2">
                    <statement><p>Deliverables 2 and 3: with the multimeter set to
                    resistance, measure the photocell covered and then lit by your
                    flashlight, and explain why the measured values might disagree with
                    the datasheet's.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d4">
                    <statement><p>Deliverable 4: compute <m>R_2</m> from your two measured
                    values.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d5">
                    <statement><p>Deliverables 5 and 6: using the divider formula with
                    <m>V_{cc} = 3.3</m> V and your measured values, work out the voltages
                    you expect at <m>V_1</m> with the cell dark and with it lit, first
                    with your computed <m>R_2</m> and then with the kit's 10 kΩ, and
                    decide whether the 10 kΩ is a problem.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                Now we'll build the circuit.  Lab 8's Figure 2
                (<xref ref="fig-day16-lab-fig2"/>) shows the two dividers on the
                breadboard, one node on A0 (PA0) and the other on A1 (PA1).  A0 and A1
                are ADC channels 0 and 1, ADC_IN0 and ADC_IN1 in Table 12 of the
                STM32C031C6 datasheet, <em>Pin assignment and description</em>, the same
                lookup as in the ADC chapter (<xref ref="subsec-adc-day7-datasheet"/>).
                The AD2's voltmeter, in Waveforms, reads both nodes at once, before any
                program runs, so that a wrong reading can be traced to the circuit rather
                than to the program.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-day16-lab-fig2">
                <caption>Lab 8's Figure 2, the two photocell dividers on the breadboard.
                The Nucleo's 3.3 V and GND feed the two rails.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/slide09_a9c74b15.png" width="55%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-dividers">
                <title>Wire the two dividers</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-wire-dividers">
                    <statement><p>With nothing powered, wire the two photosensors and the
                    two 10 kΩ resistors on your breadboard as in
                    <xref ref="fig-day16-lab-fig2"/>: for each divider, the photocell from
                    the 3.3 V rail to a node row and the resistor from that row to the
                    ground rail, and a wire from the node row to A0 (PA0) for one divider
                    and to A1 (PA1) for the other.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-voltmeter">
                    <statement><p>Connect the AD2's channel 1, the orange lead, to one
                    node and channel 2, the blue lead, to the other, and both striped
                    leads to the ground rail.  Plug in the USB cable, open Waveforms'
                    voltmeter, and check both readings against the range you expected in
                    Deliverable 6, with the cells lit by the room and then covered.  If
                    a node reads the rail's own voltage, 3.3 V or 0 V, its photocell and
                    its resistor do not share a row.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                Now we'll modify your ADC program to read both channels.  Your ADC
                library from Lab 5 selects a channel with <c>adc_setChannel()</c> and
                reads it with <c>adc_getValue()</c>, and in Lab 5 you moved between the
                potentiometer and the temperature sensor with exactly those two calls.
                The two-channel program is the same thing twice in a loop: select
                channel 0 and read, select channel 1 and read, print both, once a second.
                If your library uses other names for those functions, use your own; and
                if you start from your servo program instead, note that it reads one
                fixed channel, so bring <c>adc_setChannel()</c> over from Lab 5.
                <xref ref="subsec-photo-ref-two-channel"/> has what the Reference Manual
                says about changing the channel.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-two-channel">
                <title>Print both channels, Deliverables 7 and 8</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-program">
                    <statement><p>Modify your Lab 5 program, or the ADC part of your servo
                    program, to read channel 0 and then channel 1 with your library and
                    print both values once a second.  Build, run, and open CoolTerm.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d7">
                    <statement><p>Deliverable 7: compare each printed value, converted to a
                    voltage, with the AD2's reading of the same node and with the range
                    you expected.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d8">
                    <statement><p>Deliverable 8: slowly sweep the flashlight beam across the
                    two sensors and observe how the two voltages change.  Note the
                    maximum and the minimum, and compare them with your theoretical
                    maximum and minimum.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                <term>Both channels print the same number?</term>  Look at the AD2 first.
                If the two nodes read differently on the voltmeter and the same in the
                printout, the program is reading one channel twice: check that the
                channel is set before each read, and that the two calls name different
                channels.  If the two nodes also read the same on the voltmeter, the two
                dividers share a node row on the breadboard.  A value that drifts or
                makes no sense while the voltmeter reads a sensible node means the
                program is reading a channel other than the one your wire is on.
            </p>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day16-two-channel">
                <p>The two-channel loop, with Lab 5's names.  The delay is fine for the
                study; the tracker's loop replaces it with <c>milliseconds()</c>.</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    while (1) {
        adc_setChannel(0);
        v0 = adc_getValue();          // the divider on A0 (PA0)
        adc_setChannel(1);
        v1 = adc_getValue();          // the divider on A1 (PA1)
        printf("A0=%d  A1=%d\n\r", v0, v1);
        delay_ms(1000);
    }
]]></code></program>
                <p>A library whose <c>adc_setChannel()</c> writes <c>CHSELR</c> without
                waiting for <c>CCRDY</c> has its <c>ADSTART</c> write ignored (RM0490,
                <c>ADC_CR</c>), so no conversion starts: the read that follows either
                blocks on <c>EOC</c> or returns the previous conversion's result, which
                is the old channel's, one read late.  A student whose two values look
                swapped, or lag the light by one sample, has that; the fix is the wait,
                in the Reference section.  PA1 needs no <c>MODER</c> change:
                <c>GPIOA_MODER</c> resets to 0xEBFFFFFF (RM0490 §6.4.1), so every
                analog-capable pin starts in analog mode; Day 7 set PA0 explicitly, so
                this will be asked.  Deliverable 1's rows, for checking: dark resistance
                0.2 MΩ minimum after 10 s at 10 lux; illuminated resistance 3 kΩ to
                11 kΩ at 10 lux.</p>
            </instructor>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day16-part2-checkpoint">
                <p>Two checkpoints in this Part, about minute 39 (after the dividers are
                wired and on the voltmeter) and minute 53 (after the program).  Minute 53
                is a hard stop: a student who is not printing both channels finishes
                Deliverables 7 and 8 as the first step of Lab 8 rather than holding the
                room.  A student whose servo did not follow the knob at the opening check
                goes on with the study anyway: nothing in this Part or the next needs
                the servo, and the arm's electrical work in Part 4 does not either.  The
                dark measurement for Deliverable 2 is taken with the cell covered.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Minute 39.  A node at 3.3 V or 0 V: the photocell and its resistor
                    are in different rows, or the jumper from the rail is in the wrong
                    row.  A node that does not change when the cell is covered: the cell's
                    leads are not both in the breadboard.  Two nodes that read the same:
                    the two dividers share a row.</li>
                    <li>Minute 53.  Two channels that print the same value while the
                    voltmeter reads two different nodes: the channel is not being
                    changed between reads, or the second call selects the same channel.
                    Values that are swapped relative to the AD2: the wires to A0 and A1
                    are exchanged, which does not matter for the tracker, or the missing
                    CCRDY wait above.  A value that drifts, or makes no sense against a
                    sensible voltmeter reading, with no matching fault on the breadboard:
                    the program is reading a channel other than the one on the
                    node.</li>
                </ul>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3: 5 min = 3 (the family) + 2 (rise and fall times, the servo's frame, against T) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day16-family">
            <title>Part 3: One Circuit for a Family of Sensors</title>
            <p>
                The circuit you just built reads any sensor whose resistance changes
                with the quantity we want: a thermistor for temperature, a force-sensing
                resistor for force, a photoresistor for light, and the potentiometer for
                position or angle, each one in the same divider and each on one ADC
                channel.  What changes from sensor to sensor is the fixed resistor,
                chosen by the same geometric-mean reasoning from that sensor's own range,
                and the shape of that sensor's own nonlinear curve, read from its own
                datasheet.  The potentiometer is the exception you have already built:
                its track is both halves of the divider, so it needs no fixed resistor
                at all.
            </p>
            <p>
                How fast the tracker can usefully read its sensors is set by the
                photocell's own speed.  The PDV-P8001's rise time is 55 ms and its fall
                time 20 ms, both at 10 lux: after the light changes, the resistance takes
                tens of milliseconds to respond.  Lab 8's one-second sampling for the
                study is far slower than that, and the tenth of a second we would choose
                for the loop is still longer than either time, so neither the study nor
                the loop is limited by the cell.  The other bound is the servo.  Its
                datasheet gives 0.1 s per 60°, so a correction of a few degrees takes
                only a few milliseconds to complete, and in any case the servo accepts a
                new command once per 20 ms frame; sampling much faster than the frame
                buys nothing.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4: 14 min = 7 (cups and clips) + 4 (both channels from the arm, the sweep)
             + 3 (checkpoint at minute 72) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day16-arm">
            <title>Part 4: The Photocells on the Arm</title>
            <p>
                Now we'll add the photocells to the tracker arm of your servo.  The arm
                has a shielding cup at each end, and a photocell is inserted into each
                cup (<xref ref="fig-day16-cup"/>).  The clip leads go into the same
                rows the photocells came out of, to recreate the photocell circuit from
                earlier.  The end state is <xref ref="fig-day16-full-setup"/>: the two
                dividers, the servo on the regulator board's 5 V, and no potentiometer.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-day16-cup">
                <caption>A photocell in the shielding cup at one end of the tracker arm.
                The cup shades the cell from light arriving from the other side of the
                arm.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/slide11_324c26cf.png" width="55%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-day16-clips">
                <caption>Alligator-to-breadboard clips on the photocells' leads.  Each
                photocell has two leads, and each lead gets one clip; the other end of
                each clip is a pin that goes into the breadboard.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/slide12_a6a60c41.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-day16-full-setup">
                <caption>The complete tracker circuit.  The two photocells are drawn on
                the breadboard, but they are physically in the cups of your tracker arm,
                reached through the alligator clips.  Each divider is a photocell from
                the 3.3 V rail to a node row and a 10 kΩ resistor from that row to the
                ground rail; one node goes to A0 (PA0) and the other to A1 (PA1).  The
                servo is powered from the regulator board's 5V pin and its orange signal
                lead goes to D11 (PA7), as in <xref ref="fig-servo-powering"/>.  The
                regulator board is plugged into the breadboard, so each of its pins is
                reached through the row it sits in.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/week8FullLabSetup.png" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-arm">
                <title>Solar tracker assembly</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-cups">
                    <statement><p>With nothing powered, remove your photocells from the
                    breadboard and insert them into the shielding cups on the motor arm,
                    as in <xref ref="fig-day16-cup"/>.  If your photocells were already
                    in the cups, they are already wired through their clips; go on to
                    the last step.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-clips">
                    <statement><p>Use alligator-to-breadboard clips to make the electrical
                    connections between the leads of the photosensors and your
                    breadboard, one clip per lead (<xref ref="fig-day16-clips"/>).</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-recreate">
                    <statement><p>Connect the pins of the alligator clips to your
                    breadboard to recreate the photocell circuit from earlier: each
                    photocell's two clips go into the row of its red jumper from the
                    3.3 V rail and the row of its node, as in
                    <xref ref="fig-day16-full-setup"/>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-arm-print">
                    <statement><p>Power up in the usual order and run your two-channel
                    program.  Both channels print, now from the arm.  Then sweep the
                    flashlight slowly across the arm from one side to the other and note
                    which channel rises as the light moves toward which side of the arm.
                    Together with which way the arm turned as the knob raised the
                    reading, that is what the loop's sign is found from.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                <term>A channel changed when the clips went on?</term>  A channel that
                now prints a rail value, 4095 or 0, or no longer changes with the light,
                has a clip on the wrong row or a lead that is not held in its clip.  Two
                channels that print the same value have their two clips in one row.
            </p>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day16-arm-checkpoint">
                <p>Checkpoint about minute 72.  The arm changes no electrical node, so
                every fault here is a clip: on the wrong row, in a rail, or not gripping
                the lead.  A channel at 4095 with its clip in the 3.3 V jumper's row and
                nothing in the node row is the one to look for first.  A student whose
                servo is not yet working still does this Part; the servo is not needed
                until the loop runs.  The sign observation (which channel rises as the
                light moves which way, and which way the arm turned with the knob at
                the opening check) is what Part 5's first question uses.  Part 5 can
                open with the tables that have both facts while the last tables finish
                their clips during the read-section-4 beat; one instructor cannot verify
                thirty tables' clips before starting.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5: 22 min = 2 (the two loops) + 3 (read §4) + 4 (commit 1, the sign)
             + 5 (commit 2, the integer step) + 3 (commit 3, K and T too large)
             + 1 (the lab's tuning paragraph) + 4 (the bounds, milliseconds()) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day16-loop">
            <title>Part 5: The Feedback Loop We Write</title>
            <p>
                There are two feedback loops in the tracker
                (<xref ref="fig-tracker-loop-servo"/> and
                <xref ref="fig-tracker-loop-around"/>).  The one inside the servo is the
                servo chapter's: the command position goes in, the potentiometer on the
                output shaft measures the position, and the servo's own controller drives
                the motor until the error is zero.  That loop is built into the servo
                and we do not program it.  The one around the servo is Lab 8's Figure 6:
                the two sensors, the two ADC channels, a controller, the PWM pulse, the
                servo, and the angle of the arm, which sets what the two sensors receive
                and so closes the loop through the light itself.  The controller in
                this loop is the program you will write in Lab 8.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tracker-loop-servo">
                <caption>The loop inside the servo, from the servo chapter: the command
                position, the error, the controller, the motor, the gears, and the
                potentiometer that feeds the position back.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/slide08_095020b6.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tracker-loop-around">
                <caption>Lab 8's Figure 6, the loop around the servo: two sensors, two
                ADC conversions, the controller we write, the PWM pulse, the servo, and
                the angle θ of the arm, which feeds back to the sensors through the
                light.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/lab8-fig6-tracker-loop.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-read-tracker">
                <title>Read "A Solar Tracker" in the lab handout</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Read the start of Lab 8's section 4: the block diagram and the
                    paragraph that begins <q>Here is the theory of operation</q>, through
                    the two equations for PWM(t + T).  Stop before the paragraph that
                    begins <q>You, as the designer</q>.</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <p>
                In Lab 8's notation the two sensor voltages are <m>V_0</m> and
                <m>V_1</m>, the ADC readings of the two channels (Lab 8 reuses
                <m>V_1</m> here for the second arm channel, not for Figure 1's
                test-divider node); the error is <m>e = V_1 - V_0</m>; and the
                controller updates the PWM value every sampling interval <m>T</m>,
                either by a fixed step, <m>\text{PWM}(t + T) = \text{PWM}(t) \pm \Delta</m>,
                or by a step proportional to the error,
                <m>\text{PWM}(t + T) = \text{PWM}(t) + K\,e(t)</m>, with <m>K</m> the
                gain.  Discuss at your table how you might implement the feedback loop,
                in three steps.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-design">
                <title>Discuss at your table how you might implement the feedback loop</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-sign">
                    <statement><p>Suppose <m>V_1</m> is larger than <m>V_0</m>, so that
                    <m>e</m> is positive.  Which way should the arm turn, and should the
                    PWM value increase or decrease to turn it that way?  What do you need
                    to know about your own build to answer that?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-integer">
                    <statement><p>Lab 8 recommends starting with <m>K</m> on the order of
                    0.01, with <m>e</m> in ADC counts and the PWM value in timer counts.
                    With <m>e = 50</m>, what step does the arithmetic give, in whole
                    timer counts?  For which values of <m>e</m> is the step zero?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-tuning">
                    <statement><p>Predict what the arm does if <m>K</m> is much too large,
                    and what it does if <m>T</m> is much too long.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                We'll take the sign first.  A positive error means the photocell on
                channel 1 receives more light than the one on channel 0, so the arm has
                to turn toward channel 1's side.  Whether that is a larger or a smaller
                PWM value depends on two things that are properties of your build and
                not of Lab 8: which photocell is on A1, and which way the arm turns as
                the pulse gets wider, which you saw as you turned the knob.  Both are
                what you noted from your own board earlier today, so the sign of
                <m>K</m> comes from your board and not from the book.
            </p>
            <p>
                Next comes the size of the step.  The step <m>K e</m> has to be a whole
                number of timer counts, because CCR1 holds an integer.  With
                <m>K = 0.01</m> and <m>e = 50</m> the product is 0.5 counts, and integer
                arithmetic gives 0.  If <m>K</m> is declared as an <c>int</c>, 0.01 is
                already 0 before it is multiplied by anything, and every step is 0.
                Written as a division instead, a step of <m>e/100</m>, the step is zero
                until <m>|e|</m> reaches 100: every error below 100 counts, which is
                about 0.08 V on the ADC's 3.3 V, 12-bit scale (100 counts × 0.8 mV per
                count = 80 mV), produces no correction at all.  That band is a
                <term>dead zone</term>.  Inside it the loop does not correct, and the
                arm rests wherever it is.  Keeping the arithmetic in integers is a
                trade: a smaller and faster loop with no floating-point library, at the
                price of a dead zone whose width is set by <m>K</m>.  The loop in Lab 8
                uses the ADC counts directly, and <xref ref="subsec-photo-ref-loop"/>
                says what Lab 8's <q>lump VREF/4096 into K</q> means.
            </p>
            <p>
                There are two ways this loop can misbehave.  With <m>K</m> too large,
                each step overshoots the balance point, the error changes sign, the next
                step overshoots back, and the arm hunts around the light instead of
                settling on it.  With <m>T</m> too long, the arm corrects only every
                <m>T</m>, and a moving light is always ahead of it, so the arm lags.
                Lab 8's next paragraph, the one that begins <q>You, as the
                designer</q>, describes the same two failure modes and recommends
                starting cautiously, with <m>K</m> on the order of 0.01, and increasing
                it as you gain understanding of the system's dynamics.
            </p>
            <p>
                In Lab 8 you are also asked to impose upper and lower bounds on the PWM
                value before it is written to <c>CCR1</c>, TIM14's compare register, so
                that the servo is not driven beyond ±60° of center.  Your servo program
                from Day 15 already applies a limit in <c>updateServo()</c> before every
                write; its <c>SERVO_MIN</c> and <c>SERVO_MAX</c> are the 1 ms and 2 ms
                endpoints, and Lab 8's ±60° is narrower than that, so the two numbers
                are yours to work out from the pulse convention
                (<xref ref="subsec-servo-ref-command"/>).  You are also asked to keep
                track of <m>T</m> with <c>milliseconds()</c>, the SysTick count from the
                motor chapter (<xref ref="subsec-day12-main-loop"/>).  The loop then has
                the shape of the Day 12 main loop, and it takes two lines:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    if (milliseconds() - last_update >= T_MS) {
        last_update = milliseconds();
        // read both channels, compute e, update the PWM value, bound it, write it
    }
]]></code></program>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day16-loop">
                <p>A worked loop, in the shape Lab 8 describes, with Day 15's
                <c>updateServo()</c> and Lab 5's ADC names.  The sign of the step and
                the two bound values are the student's; <c>K_DIV</c> of 100 is Lab 8's
                K = 0.01 as a division.  <c>pwm</c> and <c>e</c> are signed,
                because <c>e</c> is negative half the time.</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define T_MS        100         // sampling interval T, in ms
#define K_DIV       100         // K = 1/K_DIV = 0.01
#define PWM_LOWER   /* their value: 60 degrees below center */
#define PWM_UPPER   /* their value: 60 degrees above center */

    int32_t  v0, v1, e;
    int32_t  pwm = SERVO_MID;
    uint32_t last_update = milliseconds();

    while (1) {
        if (milliseconds() - last_update >= T_MS) {
            last_update = milliseconds();
            adc_setChannel(0);
            v0 = adc_getValue();
            adc_setChannel(1);
            v1 = adc_getValue();
            e = v1 - v0;
            pwm = pwm + e / K_DIV;              // or pwm - e / K_DIV: the sign is found on the board
            if (pwm > PWM_UPPER) pwm = PWM_UPPER;
            if (pwm < PWM_LOWER) pwm = PWM_LOWER;
            updateServo((uint16_t)pwm);
        }
    }
]]></code></program>
                <p>The fixed-step version replaces the update with
                <c>if (e &gt; 0) pwm = pwm + STEP; else if (e &lt; 0) pwm = pwm - STEP;</c>,
                which has no dead zone and, because <c>e</c> is almost never exactly
                zero, dithers by one step about the balance point.  What to expect on
                the demo: with K_DIV of 100 the arm settles inside a band of about 100
                counts of error and stops; halving K_DIV halves the band and, past some
                value, starts the hunting from commit 3.  A competing light, Lab 8's
                warning, shows up as an arm that settles pointing between the flashlight
                and a window or a laptop screen.  A student whose servo still does not
                run pairs with a tablemate's board for the sign observation, predicts the
                sign from the pulse convention alone, and does Part 6 as design and
                pseudocode.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 6: 11 min = 2 (Deliverables 9 and 10, the warning) + 9 (start on the loop) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day16-start">
            <title>Part 6: Starting the Tracker</title>
            <p>
                We'll spend the rest of today starting on Lab 8's design challenge, which
                is your lab work.  In Lab 8, Deliverable 9 is a demonstration of the
                working tracker, with a log of your tuning of <m>K</m> and <m>T</m>, and
                Deliverable 10 puts the seven-segment display back into the system to
                show the servo's angle.  When you test your system make sure that your
                flashlight is brighter than any competing light source; a laptop screen,
                a lamp across the room and a sunny window all attract the tracker.  If
                your arm is not wired yet, plan the loop in words now and build it as
                the first step of Lab 8.
            </p>
            <p>
                The starting point is your servo program from Day 15: it already sets up
                TIM14 for the pulse, reads the ADC, and limits the value in
                <c>updateServo()</c> before every write.  What changes is that the
                potentiometer read becomes two photocell reads, the map from the knob
                becomes the update rule, and the TIM16 tick becomes
                <c>milliseconds()</c> with <m>T</m> as a <c>#define</c>.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-start">
                <title>Start on the loop</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Do these in order, and before you run anything predict whether the
                    arm will hunt, lag, or track the light with the values you chose.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-plan">
                    <statement><p>Plan the loop at your table in words before you change
                    any code: what is read, what is computed from it, what is bounded,
                    what is written, and how the loop waits for <m>T</m>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-fixed-step">
                    <statement><p>Make the fixed-step version first, Lab 8's first
                    equation, with a step of a few timer counts, and see whether the arm
                    turns toward the light or away from it.  Then the proportional
                    version, with <m>K</m> as Lab 8 recommends.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                A loop with the wrong sign drives the arm away from the light, once it
                is off balance, until it reaches a bound and holds it there, so a wrong
                sign shows up as an arm pinned at one end of its travel rather than as an
                arm that hunts.  If that is what yours does, reverse the sign of the
                step.
            </p>
            <!-- Close, 5 min.  Presenter note for the deck: Lab 8 is next; the log of K
                 and T; the competing-light warning is in the Part's first paragraph.
                 No homework is due beyond Lab 8 (Petra, 2026-09-03). -->
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-photo-reference">
        <title>Reference: The Divider, the Two-Channel Read, and the Loop</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Lookup material for the photocell and the tracker.  Nothing here is new;
                it is the same information arranged for a quick look-up.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-ref-divider">
            <title>The Divider and the Fixed Resistor</title>
            <p>
                With the sensor at the top, from <m>V_{cc}</m> to the node, and the
                fixed resistor <m>R_M</m> from the node to ground,
                <m>V_M = V_{cc} \cdot R_M/(R_\text{sens} + R_M)</m>, and <m>V_M</m> rises
                as <m>R_\text{sens}</m> falls.  With the two exchanged,
                <m>V_M = V_{cc} \cdot R_\text{sens}/(R_\text{sens} + R_M)</m>, and
                <m>V_M</m> falls as <m>R_\text{sens}</m> falls.  On the STM32C031C6,
                <m>V_{cc}</m> is 3.3 V and the 12-bit reading is
                <m>4096 \times V_M / 3.3</m>, so one count is about 0.8 mV.
            </p>
            <p>
                For a sensor whose resistance swings between <m>R_\text{low}</m> and
                <m>R_\text{high}</m>, the fixed resistor that puts the two readings
                farthest apart is their geometric mean,
                <m>R_M = \sqrt{R_\text{low} \times R_\text{high}}</m>.  The swing in
                <m>V_M</m> is
                <m>V_{cc}\,[R_M/(R_\text{low} + R_M) - R_M/(R_\text{high} + R_M)]</m>,
                which is small when <m>R_M</m> is far below both resistances (both terms
                near 0) and when it is far above both (both terms near 1); the geometric
                mean puts <m>R_\text{low}</m> and <m>R_\text{high}</m> the same factor
                below and above <m>R_M</m>, which is where the swing is largest.  A cell
                that happened to swing between 2 kΩ and 200 kΩ, not this photocell's own
                range, would have a geometric mean of <m>\sqrt{2\text{ kΩ} \times 200\text{ kΩ}} = 20</m> kΩ:
                ten times 2 kΩ, and a tenth of 200 kΩ, the same factor each way.  The
                Adafruit guide calls this the Axel Benz formula.  You build with the
                nearest standard resistor value; a value away from the geometric mean
                costs you a smaller swing between the dark and the lit reading, not a
                wrong reading.
            </p>
            <p>
                The photocell's resistance against illumination is close to a straight
                line on log-log axes, <m>R \propto E^{-S}</m>.  The PDV-P8001
                datasheet's <em>Sensitivity</em> row states <m>S</m> as
                <m>[\log(R_{100}) - \log(R_{10})] / [\log(E_{100}) - \log(E_{10})]</m>,
                with <m>R_{100}</m> and <m>R_{10}</m> the resistances at 100 lux and at
                10 lux and <m>E_{100}</m> and <m>E_{10}</m> those two illuminances, and
                gives 0.6 as the typical value.  The sheet prints the magnitude; on the
                plot the slope is negative, because <m>R</m> falls as <m>E</m> rises,
                and the relationship reads
                <m>\log_{10}(R_{100}) - \log_{10}(R_{10}) = -0.6\,[\log_{10}(E_{100}) - \log_{10}(E_{10})]</m>.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-ref-two-channel">
            <title>Reading Two Channels</title>
            <p>
                The ADC converts the channels selected in <c>ADC_CHSELR</c>, one bit per
                channel (<xref ref="fig-adc-chselr"/>).  The Lab 5 library's
                <c>adc_setChannel()</c> writes that register with a single channel's
                bit, and a read that follows converts that channel.  The Reference Manual
                adds one condition (RM0490 §14, the <c>ADC_CR</c> register description):
                after writing to <c>ADC_CHSELR</c> it is mandatory to wait until the
                <c>CCRDY</c> flag in <c>ADC_ISR</c> is set before setting
                <c>ADSTART</c>, otherwise the write to <c>ADSTART</c> is ignored.  A
                channel-select function that does not wait therefore starts no
                conversion at all: the read that follows either blocks on <c>EOC</c> or
                returns the previous conversion's result, which is the old channel's.
                The other way to read two channels is to select both bits in
                <c>ADC_CHSELR</c> at once: in that case the sequencer converts the
                selected channels in increasing channel order, which is the reset
                configuration (<c>CHSELRMOD = 0</c>, <c>SCANDIR = 0</c>; Reference
                Manual §14.4.8, <em>Channel selection</em>), the <c>EOC</c> (end of
                conversion) flag is set after each conversion with that channel's result
                in <c>ADC_DR</c>, and the <c>EOS</c> (end of sequence) flag is set after
                the last.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-ref-loop">
            <title>The Tracker's Update Rule</title>
            <p>
                In Lab 8's notation, with <m>V_0</m> and <m>V_1</m> the two ADC readings,
                the error is <m>e = V_1 - V_0</m> and the proportional update is
                <m>\text{PWM}(t + T) = \text{PWM}(t) + K\,e(t)</m>, evaluated every
                <m>T</m> milliseconds and bounded before it is written to
                <c>CCR1</c>.  Lab 8's remark that the proportionality constant
                <m>V_\text{REF}/4096</m> can be lumped into <m>K</m> means this: an
                ADC count is <m>3.3/4096</m> V, so an error in volts is the error in
                counts times that constant, and a gain that was chosen to multiply volts
                becomes, when it multiplies counts instead, that gain times the same
                constant.  Lumping <m>V_\text{REF}/4096</m> into <m>K</m> is that
                multiplication.  Since <m>K</m> is chosen by experiment, there is no
                reason to convert to volts at all; the counts are used directly, and
                <m>K</m> is chosen by trying values on your own build.
            </p>
            <p>
                In integer arithmetic a gain below 1 is a division of the error by a
                constant, with <m>K = 1/\text{K\_DIV}</m>.  Because <c>/</c> on integers
                discards the remainder, every error with
                <m>|e| &lt; \text{K\_DIV}</m> produces a step of 0, a dead zone of
                <m>\pm\text{K\_DIV}</m> counts around the balance point, about
                <m>0.8\text{ mV} \times \text{K\_DIV}</m>.  A smaller <c>K_DIV</c> is a
                larger gain and a narrower dead zone, and past some value the loop
                overshoots and hunts.  A <c>float</c> <m>K</m> removes the dead zone
                only if the PWM value is accumulated in a <c>float</c> too and converted
                to an integer at the write, since assigning 300.5 to an integer discards
                the same half count; the STM32C031C6 has no floating-point unit, so that
                arithmetic runs in software.  The error can be negative, so <c>e</c> and
                the PWM value are signed integers, <c>int32_t</c>, and the bounded value
                is converted back to <c>uint16_t</c> only when it is written.  The
                sampling interval <m>T</m> is kept with <c>milliseconds()</c>
                (<xref ref="subsec-day12-main-loop"/>); the photocell's rise and fall
                times, 55 ms and 20 ms, and the servo's 20 ms command frame put no
                useful <m>T</m> below a few tens of milliseconds.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

</chapter>
